<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS Info.plist additions. Tauri merges this into the generated bundle.

  Bluetooth is here because the Mac is a FIRST-CLASS FIRST DEVICE: it finds an
  unclaimed box over Improv/BLE and sets up its wifi, the same as the phone
  does (see `virtues_improv::client`). Without this key CoreBluetooth refuses
  the very first scan, and macOS surfaces nothing to the user — the app simply
  finds no boxes, forever, which is indistinguishable from a box that isn't
  advertising. The iOS side has carried the same key since BLE setup shipped;
  this is its desktop twin.

  Local networking covers the other half of setup: discovering an
  already-online box on the LAN (mDNS + a subnet probe) and pairing to it.

  CFBundleIconName points macOS at the `AppIcon` entry inside Assets.car, which
  is what carries the Icon Composer layers — the light / dark / clear / tinted
  renditions the system picks between. Without this key macOS falls back to the
  flat CFBundleIconFile icns and the user's appearance setting does nothing.

  Assets.car and AppIcon.icns are NOT produced by this build. They are compiled
  from icons/AppIcon.icon by `actool` and committed under
  icons/AppIcon.compiled/, then copied into Contents/Resources by
  `bundle.resources` in tauri.conf.json — which runs before signing, so the
  bundle is never modified after codesign.

  They are committed rather than compiled here for two reasons. Listing a
  `.icon` in `bundle.icon` switches tauri onto an actool path that fails
  (d9752668), and release CI runs `pnpm tauri build` directly on a macos-15
  runner rather than tools/build-mac-app.sh — so any pre-build compile step
  would silently not run where it matters most. Regenerate them with
  `make icons` after changing the mark.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleIconName</key>
    <string>AppIcon</string>
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>AppIcon</string>
    <key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Virtues uses Bluetooth to find your box during setup and put it on your Wi-Fi.</string>
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Virtues finds your box on this network so it can connect to it directly.</string>
    <key>NSBonjourServices</key>
    <array>
        <string>_virtues._tcp</string>
        <string>_http._tcp</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
